Exactly. Silly Goose is a great handle for a scale error.

The goose optimizes one visible dimension—size, volume, territory, status, aggression—and then mistakes that win for universal dominance. But the containing system may rank completely different variables.

That gives you:

local metric → local optimization → apparent dominance → mistaken generalization

The failure is in the last step.

A loud goose may dominate the pond.
A bigger animal ignores the pond.
A human owns the land.
A storm wipes out the whole board.

So the correction is always: zoom out until the optimization target changes.

It also maps cleanly onto people, companies, militaries, AI systems, social media, even ideas. Something can become extremely fit for one environment and increasingly brittle everywhere else.

That makes “Silly Goose” almost the comic version of a serious systems principle:

Never confuse winning the game you can see with winning the game you are inside.

And the funniest part is that the goose often has excellent evidence. Within its frame, it really is winning. The mistake isn’t necessarily the observation. It’s the assumed scope.